1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
Fasten your seatbelts.

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:07,000
Make sure your trays are in their upright position.

3
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:11,000
And prepare yourselves for a journey through the history of airfields.

4
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:17,000
From primitive farmers' fields through the constantly evolving and expanding multi-mile layouts of today.

5
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:21,000
Now, International Airports on Modern Marbles.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
You probably think keeping a 430 ton airplane flying is just a physics problem.

7
00:00:50,740 --> 00:00:51,740
Think again.

8
00:00:52,580 --> 00:00:55,600
The real challenge starts when the plane's back on the ground.

9
00:00:55,601 --> 00:00:59,200
Consider for a moment the difficulties facing modern

10
00:00:59,300 --> 00:01:02,840
airports as they link and shrink our busy world.

11
00:01:02,940 --> 00:01:06,840
They are models of intricately coordinated organization,

12
00:01:06,940 --> 00:01:11,240
symphonies of managed chaos and unexpected change,

13
00:01:11,340 --> 00:01:14,860
as they send more people further, faster.

14
00:01:14,960 --> 00:01:18,360
Over 72,000 of us hurl ourselves into the air on

15
00:01:18,460 --> 00:01:22,660
thousands of planes every hour of every day and safely land

16
00:01:22,661 --> 00:01:27,160
safely land anywhere from Anchorage to Zurich in less time

17
00:01:27,161 --> 00:01:29,160
than it takes to read the Sunday newspaper.

18
00:01:31,960 --> 00:01:35,160
50 years ago, it took more than a day to fly from Los Angeles

19
00:01:35,161 --> 00:01:36,860
to Japan.

20
00:01:36,861 --> 00:01:39,860
Today, a flight from LAX to the new Kansai International

21
00:01:39,861 --> 00:01:44,860
Airport takes less than 14 hours.

22
00:01:44,861 --> 00:01:48,460
Kansai features the latest trend in airport construction.

23
00:01:48,461 --> 00:01:52,460
It is built on a 5.6 million square foot man-made island

24
00:01:52,461 --> 00:01:55,900
in the middle of the ocean.

25
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:58,600
Kansai is a response to the overwhelming need

26
00:01:58,700 --> 00:02:01,000
for more airport services in a place

27
00:02:01,100 --> 00:02:04,700
where encroaching urban sprawl and a lack of available land

28
00:02:04,800 --> 00:02:10,720
make a big, noisy downtown airport undesirable.

29
00:02:10,820 --> 00:02:12,520
It's a brilliant concept to look at.

30
00:02:15,720 --> 00:02:20,520
We think, well, wouldn't we rather land closer to the action,

31
00:02:20,521 --> 00:02:22,420
closer to where we really want to be.

32
00:02:22,520 --> 00:02:25,100
The truth of the matter is we want a land where we can get

33
00:02:25,200 --> 00:02:29,060
in and out and get the minimum hassle in terms of being

34
00:02:29,160 --> 00:02:31,000
able to move through the facilities.

35
00:02:31,100 --> 00:02:35,000
And Kansai certainly offers that.

36
00:02:35,100 --> 00:02:38,140
Workers spent two years reclaiming land from the sea

37
00:02:38,240 --> 00:02:42,080
as a foundation for the airport, filling a massive rectangular

38
00:02:42,180 --> 00:02:47,080
seawall with 6,357 million cubic feet of Earth.

39
00:02:50,520 --> 00:02:55,120
They built a 2.3-mile-long two-tiered access bridge for

40
00:02:55,220 --> 00:02:57,460
trains and cars.

41
00:02:57,560 --> 00:03:01,960
Plus, they included a hotel, shopping complex, observation

42
00:03:02,060 --> 00:03:06,260
deck, pedestrian walkways, administration buildings,

43
00:03:06,360 --> 00:03:09,580
street lamps, dozens of restrooms, and everything else

44
00:03:09,680 --> 00:03:11,380
you'd find in a small city.

45
00:03:13,880 --> 00:03:18,280
First conceived back in 1968, the facility finally opened in

46
00:03:18,281 --> 00:03:24,920
opened in 1994 as Japan's first 24-hour airport, operating a 2.1

47
00:03:24,921 --> 00:03:32,120
mile-long runway and an average of 319 flights per day.

48
00:03:32,121 --> 00:03:35,160
The usual array of security measures, gates, and ticketing

49
00:03:35,161 --> 00:03:38,840
counters are housed in a terminal designed to reflect the culture

50
00:03:38,841 --> 00:03:43,480
and architecture of the region. The concept of building

51
00:03:43,481 --> 00:03:47,320
offshore airports and airports in locations where you otherwise

52
00:03:47,321 --> 00:03:53,060
might not have expected them are becoming more and more the vogue.

53
00:03:53,160 --> 00:03:56,960
There are risks, of course, to building an airport at sea.

54
00:03:57,060 --> 00:04:00,500
Engineers expected Kanzai to settle a little.

55
00:04:00,600 --> 00:04:04,340
Unfortunately, it is settling faster than engineers expected,

56
00:04:04,440 --> 00:04:07,140
at a rate of nearly four feet per year.

57
00:04:07,240 --> 00:04:09,080
And officials are scrambling for a solution.

58
00:04:12,220 --> 00:04:15,720
It hasn't been a smooth ride to Kanzai from Kitty Hawk,

59
00:04:15,721 --> 00:04:19,160
But it has been an exciting evolution.

60
00:04:19,260 --> 00:04:21,620
Aviation and airports have grown together

61
00:04:21,720 --> 00:04:25,060
into multi-billion dollar industries that do far more

62
00:04:25,160 --> 00:04:27,860
than provide planes with places to land.

63
00:04:27,960 --> 00:04:32,340
But that's how they began at the turn of the last century.

64
00:04:32,440 --> 00:04:36,300
The initial airports were little more than afterthoughts.

65
00:04:36,400 --> 00:04:39,340
They were grass fields that were out in the middle of a farmer's

66
00:04:39,440 --> 00:04:44,420
pasture, little reception areas for passengers.

67
00:04:44,421 --> 00:04:47,020
Farmers' pastures were good locations because they were

68
00:04:47,120 --> 00:04:50,420
flat, clear, and the farmer usually had reliable weather

69
00:04:50,520 --> 00:04:51,620
information for the pilots.

70
00:04:54,860 --> 00:04:57,560
Partly as a byproduct of the First World War,

71
00:04:57,660 --> 00:04:59,860
air travel caught on more quickly in Europe

72
00:04:59,960 --> 00:05:03,180
than in the United States.

73
00:05:03,280 --> 00:05:05,880
Roads and railways had been destroyed,

74
00:05:05,980 --> 00:05:08,880
and often the easiest path from one country to another

75
00:05:08,980 --> 00:05:09,680
was by air.

76
00:05:09,681 --> 00:05:12,980
air.

77
00:05:13,080 --> 00:05:17,120
The war also produced experienced pilots.

78
00:05:17,220 --> 00:05:20,420
European government subsidized airports in London, Paris, and

79
00:05:20,520 --> 00:05:24,220
Berlin, where large facilities were modeled on the grand train

80
00:05:24,320 --> 00:05:28,540
stations of the 19th century.

81
00:05:28,640 --> 00:05:32,540
Passengers merely walked from a large foyer out to a single gate

82
00:05:32,640 --> 00:05:35,640
or onto an open field.

83
00:05:35,740 --> 00:05:38,980
In America, a spectator sport full of daredevils would be

84
00:05:38,981 --> 00:05:42,280
transformed by the post office into a lucrative courier

85
00:05:42,380 --> 00:05:44,980
service for the mail.

86
00:05:45,080 --> 00:05:49,020
The airlines were getting a subsidy to carry mail

87
00:05:49,120 --> 00:05:51,320
in their very earliest stages.

88
00:05:51,420 --> 00:05:52,620
As a matter of fact, they really

89
00:05:52,720 --> 00:05:54,420
didn't want to carry passengers,

90
00:05:54,520 --> 00:05:56,000
because they didn't have to feed the mail.

91
00:05:56,100 --> 00:05:57,240
They just put it on the plane.

92
00:05:57,340 --> 00:06:00,140
It was a simple process.

93
00:06:00,240 --> 00:06:03,440
It was also a deadly one, as ill-equipped pilots

94
00:06:03,540 --> 00:06:07,740
flew unscheduled routes through unpredictable weather.

95
00:06:07,741 --> 00:06:12,780
31 of the first 40 pilots hired by the Postal Service in 1919

96
00:06:12,880 --> 00:06:16,380
died in crashes within six years.

97
00:06:16,480 --> 00:06:20,080
In response, the Postal Service opened their airmail operation

98
00:06:20,180 --> 00:06:25,300
to private contractors who bid on specific routes.

99
00:06:25,400 --> 00:06:28,000
But that would all change with Walter Folger Brown's

100
00:06:28,100 --> 00:06:32,340
appointment as Postmaster General in 1929.

101
00:06:32,440 --> 00:06:34,240
Brown encouraged commercial aviation

102
00:06:34,340 --> 00:06:37,100
by forcing ragtag independent airlines

103
00:06:37,101 --> 00:06:40,200
merge into a handful of large ones.

104
00:06:40,300 --> 00:06:44,040
When he took over, there were about 42 little

105
00:06:44,140 --> 00:06:45,600
airlines in the United States.

106
00:06:45,700 --> 00:06:49,840
He said, we want a proper national system.

107
00:06:49,940 --> 00:06:53,680
In 1927, the aviation industry was bolstered by a public

108
00:06:53,780 --> 00:06:56,780
relations event almost as monumental as the discovery

109
00:06:56,880 --> 00:07:00,360
of flight itself.

110
00:07:00,460 --> 00:07:02,900
This is Lowell Thomas in New York.

111
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:04,260
He made it.

112
00:07:04,261 --> 00:07:07,320
Charles de Lindbergh, Lucky Lindy as they call him,

113
00:07:07,760 --> 00:07:12,060
landed at Le Bourget Airport Paris at 524 this afternoon.

114
00:07:13,240 --> 00:07:16,040
Lindbergh became the first person to fly solo nonstop

115
00:07:16,041 --> 00:07:18,300
across the Atlantic, landing in Paris

116
00:07:18,301 --> 00:07:21,400
in his spirit of St. Louis on May 21st,

117
00:07:21,720 --> 00:07:24,240
thus opening the age of transatlantic international air

118
00:07:24,241 --> 00:07:28,080
travel. What Charles Lindbergh did for commercial aviation

119
00:07:28,081 --> 00:07:31,780
has been, to a large extent, overlooked.

120
00:07:31,781 --> 00:07:34,780
He'd take his spirit of St. Louis into a town.

121
00:07:34,880 --> 00:07:37,520
He'd land at the farmer's field at the edge of town,

122
00:07:37,620 --> 00:07:40,420
be escorted to the steps of City Hall.

123
00:07:40,520 --> 00:07:42,820
The mayor would come out and hand Charles Lindbergh

124
00:07:42,920 --> 00:07:47,720
a key to the city, and in most cases, a week, a month later,

125
00:07:47,820 --> 00:07:50,440
the city would float a bond for a half a million dollars

126
00:07:50,540 --> 00:07:52,540
and build an airport.

127
00:07:52,640 --> 00:07:55,340
But what exactly was an airport?

128
00:07:55,440 --> 00:07:57,240
Was it a kind of train station?

129
00:07:57,340 --> 00:07:59,340
An open field?

130
00:07:59,341 --> 00:08:04,340
As land became more scarce and more costly, it became obvious

131
00:08:04,341 --> 00:08:07,340
that you couldn't have these very large land sections dedicated

132
00:08:07,341 --> 00:08:12,340
strictly for an all-field type of airport, and so therefore,

133
00:08:12,341 --> 00:08:14,340
the whole runway concept was developed.

134
00:08:14,341 --> 00:08:17,340
But where to put the runway?

135
00:08:17,341 --> 00:08:20,340
In 1929, the Lehigh Portland Cement Company sponsored a

136
00:08:20,341 --> 00:08:25,340
competition that produced some curious results.

137
00:08:25,341 --> 00:08:28,340
One submission had airplanes landing on runways suspended

138
00:08:28,341 --> 00:08:32,340
suspended atop city skyscrapers.

139
00:08:32,440 --> 00:08:36,340
They love the idea of ridding out patterns.

140
00:08:36,440 --> 00:08:40,880
And so there's aesthetically wonderful patterns, stars,

141
00:08:40,980 --> 00:08:41,980
spokes.

142
00:08:42,080 --> 00:08:45,500
The designs that appeared in this airport competition

143
00:08:45,600 --> 00:08:49,200
are done by architects, for the most part, who had no experience

144
00:08:49,300 --> 00:08:55,000
in airport design and also never seem to have been hired since.

145
00:08:55,100 --> 00:08:57,340
Landing airplanes on top of skyscrapers

146
00:08:57,341 --> 00:09:00,740
may have been far-fetched, but landing them on water

147
00:09:00,840 --> 00:09:03,340
soon became commonplace.

148
00:09:03,440 --> 00:09:06,640
During the early 1930s, the epitome of passenger plane

149
00:09:06,740 --> 00:09:11,180
travel was the luxurious flying boat, an innovative airplane

150
00:09:11,280 --> 00:09:13,580
that used its smooth, reinforced underbelly

151
00:09:13,680 --> 00:09:17,200
to take off and land.

152
00:09:17,300 --> 00:09:20,500
All one needed was a few hundred feet of calm seas

153
00:09:20,600 --> 00:09:22,500
and a dock.

154
00:09:22,600 --> 00:09:26,400
In fact, the very first passenger airline

155
00:09:26,401 --> 00:09:29,060
actually started as a seaplane service in St. Petersburg,

156
00:09:29,160 --> 00:09:30,140
Florida.

157
00:09:30,240 --> 00:09:35,240
And in the early days, the seaplanes were very popular.

158
00:09:35,340 --> 00:09:38,340
Miami's Dinner Key became America's very first

159
00:09:38,440 --> 00:09:42,140
international airport, offering trips to southern international

160
00:09:42,240 --> 00:09:47,360
destinations on Pan American Airlines.

161
00:09:47,460 --> 00:09:51,960
In fact, the original terminal at Dinner Key was a houseboat.

162
00:09:52,060 --> 00:09:55,200
One prominent visitor to Miami's Dinner Key was New York's Mayor

163
00:09:55,201 --> 00:10:01,100
Fiorello LaGuardia, who wouldn't be satisfied until his city built the greatest airport in

164
00:10:01,101 --> 00:10:02,109
the world.

165
00:10:02,110 --> 00:10:09,780
Germany led the world in airport construction throughout the 1920s, building

166
00:10:09,781 --> 00:10:13,137
the first permanent airport in Konigsberg in 1923.

167
00:10:13,138 --> 00:10:15,420
International airports will return

168
00:10:15,421 --> 00:10:25,040
on Modern Marvels. We now return to International airports on Modern Marvels.

169
00:10:25,200 --> 00:10:30,200
International passenger service was burgeoning in Europe

170
00:10:30,300 --> 00:10:34,200
between the wars. In Hamburg, Germany, for instance,

171
00:10:34,300 --> 00:10:38,200
an innovative brick terminal was handling 62 passengers a day

172
00:10:38,300 --> 00:10:44,200
when it was completed in 1929. Efficiency and organization

173
00:10:44,300 --> 00:10:47,200
became a keystone, as architects built new airports in places

174
00:10:47,300 --> 00:10:50,200
like Germany, France, and England.

175
00:10:50,201 --> 00:10:52,940
This attention to efficient traffic flow

176
00:10:53,040 --> 00:10:55,500
is apparent at London's Gatwick Airport,

177
00:10:55,600 --> 00:11:00,340
which opened for international passenger service in 1936.

178
00:11:00,440 --> 00:11:03,740
The lovely thing about Gatwick, and it should be pointed out,

179
00:11:03,840 --> 00:11:05,740
that the original Gatwick terminal

180
00:11:05,840 --> 00:11:09,720
was a modern miracle in that it didn't take a long distance

181
00:11:09,820 --> 00:11:12,480
to get from the front door to the airplane,

182
00:11:12,580 --> 00:11:14,560
that it had a circular design, much

183
00:11:14,660 --> 00:11:17,100
like a beehive at the end of a finger

184
00:11:17,101 --> 00:11:19,500
like a beehive at the end of a finger.

185
00:11:19,600 --> 00:11:22,340
And it had covered walkways out to the airplanes.

186
00:11:22,440 --> 00:11:25,740
It had many of the elements that you see today

187
00:11:25,840 --> 00:11:28,340
in a modern airport.

188
00:11:28,440 --> 00:11:30,420
The Beehive terminal allowed a plane

189
00:11:30,520 --> 00:11:32,880
landing at Gatwick's open field runway

190
00:11:32,980 --> 00:11:36,320
to park at one of many gates rather than one or two gates

191
00:11:36,420 --> 00:11:41,220
that handled Gatwick's roughly a dozen daily flights.

192
00:11:41,320 --> 00:11:44,160
It also included telescoping canopies

193
00:11:44,260 --> 00:11:45,820
that could protrude from the terminal

194
00:11:45,821 --> 00:11:50,620
out to a loading airplane, like a spoke on a wheel.

195
00:11:50,720 --> 00:11:53,060
This innovation kept passengers protected

196
00:11:53,160 --> 00:11:58,560
from the elements, evolving into the loading ramps used today.

197
00:11:58,660 --> 00:12:03,280
Access to the Beehive was through an underground walkway.

198
00:12:03,380 --> 00:12:05,280
Critics complained that the circular gate

199
00:12:05,380 --> 00:12:07,280
left no room for expansion.

200
00:12:07,380 --> 00:12:10,280
But in fact, the design allowed for multiple beehives

201
00:12:10,380 --> 00:12:11,580
throughout the airport.

202
00:12:11,581 --> 00:12:14,880
airport.

203
00:12:14,980 --> 00:12:17,620
In America, New Jersey's Newark Airport

204
00:12:17,720 --> 00:12:22,620
was the world's busiest in 1935, with 30 flights a day.

205
00:12:22,720 --> 00:12:24,800
Handling only domestic flights, Newark

206
00:12:24,900 --> 00:12:27,320
was not an international airport.

207
00:12:27,420 --> 00:12:30,640
But it inadvertently inspired one.

208
00:12:30,740 --> 00:12:34,740
Newark Airport was the aerial gateway for New York City.

209
00:12:34,840 --> 00:12:37,880
In fact, Mayor LaGuardia had a flight on American Airlines

210
00:12:37,881 --> 00:12:42,020
from Chicago in 1937, and he landed at Newark.

211
00:12:42,120 --> 00:12:44,280
And he told the pilot, no, my ticket says New York,

212
00:12:44,380 --> 00:12:45,780
and I want you to take me there.

213
00:12:45,880 --> 00:12:47,920
So they took off from Newark and flew.

214
00:12:48,020 --> 00:12:49,660
It was a press gag, of course, but they

215
00:12:49,760 --> 00:12:52,500
flew to Floyd Bennett Field.

216
00:12:52,600 --> 00:12:56,000
Floyd Bennett Field was New York's official airport.

217
00:12:56,100 --> 00:12:58,640
But it was further from Manhattan than Newark was.

218
00:12:58,740 --> 00:13:01,240
So the Postal Service used Newark for its airmail

219
00:13:01,340 --> 00:13:02,600
into New York.

220
00:13:02,700 --> 00:13:05,940
LaGuardia wouldn't stand for that.

221
00:13:06,040 --> 00:13:07,640
Make no mistake about it.

222
00:13:07,641 --> 00:13:09,940
This airport was not going to be denied.

223
00:13:09,941 --> 00:13:12,020
Mayor LaGuardia was going to build this airport,

224
00:13:12,021 --> 00:13:14,980
and there was no question about it.

225
00:13:14,981 --> 00:13:17,640
LaGuardia hired the architects who designed Dinner Key

226
00:13:17,641 --> 00:13:19,220
in Florida.

227
00:13:19,221 --> 00:13:21,780
The Art Deco flavor of that earlier airport

228
00:13:21,781 --> 00:13:24,880
would be repeated in New York's new municipal airport,

229
00:13:24,881 --> 00:13:27,260
eight miles from the city.

230
00:13:27,261 --> 00:13:28,860
The circular marine air terminal

231
00:13:28,861 --> 00:13:31,840
was positioned on the shore to handle the luxurious seaplane

232
00:13:31,841 --> 00:13:34,040
flights.

233
00:13:34,041 --> 00:13:36,500
The planes would float over to the back of the terminal dock

234
00:13:36,501 --> 00:13:40,500
that provided walkways inside the rotunda.

235
00:13:40,600 --> 00:13:43,300
The airport also provided international travel

236
00:13:43,400 --> 00:13:47,040
via seaplane, as land-based passenger planes could not yet

237
00:13:47,140 --> 00:13:49,540
carry passengers overseas.

238
00:13:49,640 --> 00:13:51,540
The seaplanes traveled east to Europe

239
00:13:51,640 --> 00:13:54,860
with fueling stops at coastal ports in Canada, Greenland,

240
00:13:54,960 --> 00:13:56,860
and Iceland.

241
00:13:56,960 --> 00:13:59,860
New York City not only had a world-class airport,

242
00:13:59,960 --> 00:14:02,860
it had an international one.

243
00:14:02,861 --> 00:14:09,260
LaGuardia opened up the entire European landscape to scheduled

244
00:14:09,261 --> 00:14:13,260
air flights, as well as the domestic landscape to scheduled

245
00:14:13,261 --> 00:14:18,060
flights into New York. The airport also had a terminal

246
00:14:18,061 --> 00:14:22,460
for land-based domestic flights. Significantly, travelers at the

247
00:14:22,461 --> 00:14:26,060
airport were divided for greater efficiency.

248
00:14:26,061 --> 00:14:29,660
Departing passengers were upstairs. Arrival passengers came

249
00:14:29,661 --> 00:14:30,660
came downstairs.

250
00:14:30,760 --> 00:14:32,760
There was a clear definition between arrival

251
00:14:32,860 --> 00:14:34,160
and departing passengers.

252
00:14:34,260 --> 00:14:35,860
And of course, on the departure level

253
00:14:35,960 --> 00:14:39,000
were all the great services, the beautiful restaurant,

254
00:14:39,100 --> 00:14:41,600
the open terrace, they called it.

255
00:14:41,700 --> 00:14:44,900
It was a lovely affair.

256
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:47,120
New York Municipal Airport boasted

257
00:14:47,220 --> 00:14:51,520
two nearly mile-long runways, handling 250 flights

258
00:14:51,620 --> 00:14:55,320
its first summer in 1939.

259
00:14:55,420 --> 00:14:57,660
On opening day, a group of sky riders

260
00:14:57,661 --> 00:15:00,360
flew above the crowds and memorably wrote,

261
00:15:00,460 --> 00:15:02,560
name it LaGuardia.

262
00:15:02,660 --> 00:15:04,500
It took the city council a couple of years

263
00:15:04,600 --> 00:15:08,300
to rename the airport LaGuardia, but it became a hot spot soon

264
00:15:08,400 --> 00:15:10,400
after it opened.

265
00:15:10,500 --> 00:15:13,300
The observation decks were busy day and night watching

266
00:15:13,400 --> 00:15:14,920
the airplanes come and go.

267
00:15:15,020 --> 00:15:17,020
Barbershops were here in the 30s.

268
00:15:17,120 --> 00:15:20,220
There were small coffee shops and intimate, cozy little areas

269
00:15:20,320 --> 00:15:25,160
at the airport to sit and have a quick drink before a flight.

270
00:15:25,260 --> 00:15:27,060
What the mayor and others who created

271
00:15:27,061 --> 00:15:31,060
couldn't have anticipated was the rapid expansion of passenger

272
00:15:31,160 --> 00:15:35,000
airplane travel over the next decade.

273
00:15:35,100 --> 00:15:38,000
Air travel demand greatly increased, especially after World

274
00:15:38,100 --> 00:15:41,500
War II, and LaGuardia was clearly inadequate to handle

275
00:15:41,600 --> 00:15:46,020
the volume of flights, both domestic and international.

276
00:15:46,120 --> 00:15:48,020
New York needed another airport.

277
00:15:52,020 --> 00:15:55,760
World War II had stimulated the development of larger, faster,

278
00:15:55,761 --> 00:16:01,100
faster, more efficient aircraft that could also travel further.

279
00:16:01,200 --> 00:16:04,700
Redesigned after the war, these new multi-engine planes

280
00:16:04,800 --> 00:16:08,140
not only carried more passengers on longer trips,

281
00:16:08,240 --> 00:16:11,080
they sounded the death knell of the elegant but inefficient

282
00:16:11,180 --> 00:16:16,320
flying boats, which carried only about a dozen passengers.

283
00:16:16,420 --> 00:16:18,880
These new aircraft demanded more from the airports

284
00:16:18,980 --> 00:16:20,580
that serviced them.

285
00:16:20,680 --> 00:16:23,960
They sacrificed the ability to take off

286
00:16:23,961 --> 00:16:26,900
quickly in a short space.

287
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:30,460
But by having longer runways and getting up

288
00:16:30,560 --> 00:16:33,000
with much higher speed to take off,

289
00:16:33,100 --> 00:16:35,280
they could carry heavier loads.

290
00:16:35,380 --> 00:16:39,040
And the length of runways began to grow.

291
00:16:39,140 --> 00:16:41,640
And by the end of the Second World War,

292
00:16:41,740 --> 00:16:43,840
you had to have at least a mile of runway

293
00:16:43,940 --> 00:16:50,920
if you were going to hope to be recognized as a class airfield.

294
00:16:51,020 --> 00:16:52,760
Airports in Europe also expanded

295
00:16:52,761 --> 00:16:54,960
to accommodate the new planes.

296
00:16:55,060 --> 00:16:57,860
Amsterdam's 30-year-old Schiphol International Airport,

297
00:16:57,960 --> 00:17:00,300
for instance, was destroyed during the war,

298
00:17:00,400 --> 00:17:07,400
but rebuilt and reopened in 1946, ready for the larger planes.

299
00:17:07,500 --> 00:17:09,600
In addition to refurbished airports,

300
00:17:09,700 --> 00:17:12,920
brand new airports, like England's Heathrow, 15 miles

301
00:17:13,020 --> 00:17:16,520
from London, met the international demand.

302
00:17:16,620 --> 00:17:18,520
Within its first year of operation,

303
00:17:18,620 --> 00:17:21,360
airport planners speculated building nine runways

304
00:17:21,361 --> 00:17:23,600
to handle all the traffic.

305
00:17:23,700 --> 00:17:27,300
They ultimately built three.

306
00:17:27,400 --> 00:17:29,800
While Heathrow was being built overseas,

307
00:17:29,900 --> 00:17:32,200
in America, New York International Airport

308
00:17:32,300 --> 00:17:36,500
opened in 1948 in Jamaica Bay at the southeast end of Long

309
00:17:36,600 --> 00:17:39,620
Island, 15 miles from the city.

310
00:17:39,720 --> 00:17:42,920
Later renamed John F. Kennedy International Airport,

311
00:17:43,020 --> 00:17:44,920
it was enormous.

312
00:17:45,020 --> 00:17:48,120
Well, you could take all of LaGuardia Airport

313
00:17:48,121 --> 00:17:52,760
and fit it very neatly inside the passenger terminal oval

314
00:17:52,860 --> 00:17:53,660
at Kennedy.

315
00:17:53,760 --> 00:17:55,220
There's 600 acres here.

316
00:17:55,320 --> 00:17:57,360
There's 5,000 acres there.

317
00:17:57,460 --> 00:18:00,160
So there was the feeling that there was an endless amount

318
00:18:00,260 --> 00:18:03,360
of room to grow.

319
00:18:03,460 --> 00:18:05,540
But runway length was not the only difference

320
00:18:05,640 --> 00:18:08,300
between LaGuardia and JFK.

321
00:18:08,400 --> 00:18:10,240
Instead of a single passenger terminal

322
00:18:10,340 --> 00:18:14,720
serving a host of airlines, JFK pioneered the airport city

323
00:18:14,820 --> 00:18:18,120
concept, with each airplane building its own terminal.

324
00:18:18,121 --> 00:18:21,520
It also had its own police department and its own electrical

325
00:18:21,620 --> 00:18:23,260
power plants.

326
00:18:23,360 --> 00:18:26,520
The most interesting thing about JFK International

327
00:18:26,620 --> 00:18:30,060
Airport was here was the ultimate expression of the

328
00:18:30,160 --> 00:18:33,560
airlines versus the railroads and the steamships.

329
00:18:33,660 --> 00:18:36,880
Every one of the carriers went out to that field and built

330
00:18:36,980 --> 00:18:38,580
their own statement.

331
00:18:38,680 --> 00:18:43,180
The buildings were designed with flair and a sense of destination

332
00:18:43,280 --> 00:18:45,980
and a sense of excitement.

333
00:18:45,981 --> 00:18:50,980
The 150-foot control tower completed in 1952 was one of

334
00:18:51,080 --> 00:18:54,980
the earliest freestanding control towers, coordinating

335
00:18:55,080 --> 00:18:56,920
roughly 50 flights a day.

336
00:18:59,520 --> 00:19:02,740
JFK was America's busiest international airport

337
00:19:02,840 --> 00:19:04,840
throughout the 1950s.

338
00:19:04,940 --> 00:19:08,740
But the introduction of passenger jet aircraft in 1958

339
00:19:08,840 --> 00:19:11,240
meant bigger planes and more flights.

340
00:19:11,340 --> 00:19:15,980
Airports would have to adjust to the jet age.

341
00:19:15,981 --> 00:19:20,980
In 1946, the world's busiest airport was Amsterdam's Schiphol

342
00:19:20,981 --> 00:19:21,980
International Airport.

343
00:19:21,981 --> 00:19:26,980
With 350,000 passengers a year, International Airports will

344
00:19:26,981 --> 00:19:27,980
return on Modern Marvels.

345
00:19:34,980 --> 00:19:39,980
We now return to International Airports on Modern Marvels.

346
00:19:39,981 --> 00:19:43,980
The technology of flight advanced yet again in the 1950s,

347
00:19:43,981 --> 00:19:51,540
50s when passenger jets sent a shockwave through the community of airport architects.

348
00:19:51,541 --> 00:19:59,660
Bigger, faster, heavier planes required leaner, cleaner, more efficient jet-age airports.

349
00:19:59,661 --> 00:20:05,920
Jet aircraft technology allowed the aircraft to become larger and larger because of the

350
00:20:05,921 --> 00:20:08,520
thrust-to-weight ratios.

351
00:20:08,521 --> 00:20:11,620
And as the aircraft got larger, being able to get the aircraft

352
00:20:11,621 --> 00:20:15,660
into a gate without touching wings with an airplane that

353
00:20:15,661 --> 00:20:19,500
might be sitting in the next gate over became a real problem.

354
00:20:22,460 --> 00:20:24,640
So did the noise.

355
00:20:24,641 --> 00:20:26,940
Jets were louder than propellers.

356
00:20:26,941 --> 00:20:29,680
Airports needed more room to land the bigger planes,

357
00:20:29,681 --> 00:20:31,480
and they needed to be far enough from the city

358
00:20:31,481 --> 00:20:34,320
to avoid noise pollution.

359
00:20:34,321 --> 00:20:39,400
yet they had to be close enough to be convenient.

360
00:20:39,401 --> 00:20:42,560
Larger, more efficient planes meant more cost-efficient

361
00:20:42,561 --> 00:20:43,520
flights.

362
00:20:43,521 --> 00:20:48,440
As a result, air travel opened up to even more consumers.

363
00:20:48,441 --> 00:20:52,240
The success of the jet is not so much the speed

364
00:20:52,241 --> 00:20:55,740
that it offered, but that it reduced costs so dramatically.

365
00:20:55,741 --> 00:20:58,240
And when the seat mile costs go down,

366
00:20:58,241 --> 00:21:02,980
then that enables a volume of traffic.

367
00:21:02,981 --> 00:21:06,320
invites a new community.

368
00:21:06,321 --> 00:21:10,680
Commercial jet service began at London's Heathrow in 1952,

369
00:21:10,681 --> 00:21:13,820
six years before any other international airport,

370
00:21:13,821 --> 00:21:17,120
with flights to Johannesburg, South Africa.

371
00:21:17,121 --> 00:21:20,900
In 1953, passengers through Heathrow topped the million

372
00:21:20,901 --> 00:21:24,140
mark, the first airport to do so.

373
00:21:24,141 --> 00:21:27,840
Three years later, Heathrow saw three million.

374
00:21:27,841 --> 00:21:30,800
The first international airport in America built specifically

375
00:21:30,801 --> 00:21:35,500
for the jet age was Washington's Dulles, opened in 1962,

376
00:21:35,600 --> 00:21:37,100
and designed by Aero Saarinen.

377
00:21:44,540 --> 00:21:47,420
Three runways at Dulles were constructed of reinforced

378
00:21:47,520 --> 00:21:50,480
poured concrete, each over two miles long.

379
00:21:55,700 --> 00:21:57,420
A modern air traffic control tower

380
00:21:57,421 --> 00:22:01,920
was constructed to a height of 177 feet, 27 feet higher

381
00:22:02,020 --> 00:22:03,120
than JFK's tower.

382
00:22:07,160 --> 00:22:10,800
Dulles also pioneered the use of traveling lounges, or people

383
00:22:10,900 --> 00:22:15,840
movers, to ferry passengers more efficiently to their gates.

384
00:22:15,940 --> 00:22:18,900
Instead of trying to put a lot of people in a gate situation

385
00:22:19,000 --> 00:22:20,980
and jam them in, you could move people

386
00:22:21,080 --> 00:22:23,120
onto these people movers that would take them out

387
00:22:23,220 --> 00:22:26,680
to the aircraft waiting to board to take off.

388
00:22:26,681 --> 00:22:29,720
So it attempted several different technologies, which

389
00:22:29,820 --> 00:22:31,580
have become, in one way or another,

390
00:22:31,680 --> 00:22:36,320
commonplace at airports all over the world.

391
00:22:36,420 --> 00:22:39,560
Situated more than 25 miles from Washington, DC,

392
00:22:39,660 --> 00:22:42,260
on 10,000 acres, the airport was

393
00:22:42,360 --> 00:22:45,300
planned for rural Virginia to reduce noise pollution

394
00:22:45,400 --> 00:22:47,600
and provide the extra room required by jets.

395
00:22:50,300 --> 00:22:53,180
However, communities soon sprouted up near Dulles,

396
00:22:53,280 --> 00:22:55,300
as they often do around airports,

397
00:22:55,301 --> 00:22:56,960
to take advantage of opportunities

398
00:22:57,060 --> 00:22:59,540
the dramatic new airport would inevitably generate.

399
00:23:02,840 --> 00:23:05,540
Saarinen's startling design included a roofline

400
00:23:05,640 --> 00:23:10,440
that swept dramatically up in a metaphor of flight.

401
00:23:10,540 --> 00:23:12,860
You want to have sort of that aerodynamic flow,

402
00:23:12,960 --> 00:23:17,460
if you will, lots of light, lots of open space,

403
00:23:17,560 --> 00:23:20,360
sort of the embodiment, again, of blue skies,

404
00:23:20,460 --> 00:23:23,760
and the sky is the limit.

405
00:23:23,761 --> 00:23:26,020
The terminal at Dulles International Airport

406
00:23:26,021 --> 00:23:30,360
was undeniably, unequivocally, unabashedly an airport.

407
00:23:32,960 --> 00:23:35,580
As Dulles was making an architectural impression

408
00:23:35,581 --> 00:23:38,760
in the east, another jet-age airport in the west

409
00:23:38,761 --> 00:23:41,140
was doing its best to keep a low profile.

410
00:23:42,980 --> 00:23:45,820
Los Angeles International Airport, or LAX,

411
00:23:46,180 --> 00:23:49,120
began in the teens as an airfield in the bean fields

412
00:23:49,121 --> 00:23:51,600
18 miles west of downtown LA,

413
00:23:51,601 --> 00:23:54,387
but quickly grew after the war.

414
00:23:54,388 --> 00:23:59,600
Expanding into an international airport in the late 1950s,

415
00:24:00,040 --> 00:24:03,873
it took a radically different approach to architecture than Dulles.

416
00:24:03,874 --> 00:24:04,960
I've often referred

417
00:24:04,961 --> 00:24:08,973
to this airport as an airport without any exterior architecture.

418
00:24:08,974 --> 00:24:10,980
When you drive in, the buildings

419
00:24:10,981 --> 00:24:16,800
on either side are one and two stories, and you see a road and you see glass fronts, and you don't

420
00:24:16,801 --> 00:24:21,540
see any structures. With the strong Los Angeles car

421
00:24:21,541 --> 00:24:24,700
culture, the architects anticipated lots of vehicles

422
00:24:24,701 --> 00:24:29,240
coming to the airport and designed accordingly.

423
00:24:29,241 --> 00:24:32,720
Traffic is mitigated by LAX's innovative horseshoe-designed

424
00:24:32,721 --> 00:24:37,820
roadway that passes separate terminals aligned in a row.

425
00:24:37,821 --> 00:24:41,800
The reason for this in Los Angeles is that we did not want

426
00:24:41,801 --> 00:24:45,760
to make the passenger walk further than 40 feet from where

427
00:24:45,761 --> 00:24:50,160
he unloaded his baggage at the curb to the face of the ticket

428
00:24:50,161 --> 00:24:53,560
counter. Within the Horseshoe

429
00:24:53,561 --> 00:24:57,360
Roadway were parking lots, which could and would be expanded into

430
00:24:57,361 --> 00:25:01,760
multi-level parking garages. The design was so effective at

431
00:25:01,761 --> 00:25:05,160
controlling traffic that it took some observers by surprise.

432
00:25:05,161 --> 00:25:11,560
When we opened in 1961, the LA Times especially came out and

433
00:25:11,561 --> 00:25:18,940
photographed the empty streets and talked about the fact that we'd overspent the $20 million

434
00:25:18,941 --> 00:25:20,170
for the terminal.

435
00:25:20,171 --> 00:25:25,160
In the ensuing 20 years, population and travel went up, airplanes got

436
00:25:25,161 --> 00:25:28,206
bigger, and more people were traveling.

437
00:25:28,207 --> 00:25:31,720
Then in 1982, it was we built a second level.

438
00:25:33,460 --> 00:25:38,600
Los Angeles improved on the old LaGuardia concept of dividing, arriving, and departing

439
00:25:38,601 --> 00:25:41,640
travelers by separating them before they even got out of

440
00:25:41,641 --> 00:25:44,000
their cars.

441
00:25:44,001 --> 00:25:46,880
At LAX, departing travelers are sent to the upper level

442
00:25:46,881 --> 00:25:50,740
roadway and arrivals to the lower roadway.

443
00:25:50,741 --> 00:25:53,680
Observers were once again surprised at the success

444
00:25:53,681 --> 00:25:55,720
of the scheme.

445
00:25:55,721 --> 00:26:00,920
So when we opened the new second level roadway,

446
00:26:00,921 --> 00:26:03,100
they were out again with the mobile units.

447
00:26:03,101 --> 00:26:05,700
But the story was that, gee, we're sorry, folks.

448
00:26:05,701 --> 00:26:07,660
We're not going to be able to come out here anymore

449
00:26:07,661 --> 00:26:10,460
and report on the traffic jails because there aren't any.

450
00:26:10,461 --> 00:26:13,060
But that was almost 20 years ago now,

451
00:26:13,061 --> 00:26:16,020
and we're back to the point where traffic is overloading

452
00:26:16,021 --> 00:26:17,260
the second level roadway.

453
00:26:19,960 --> 00:26:21,860
LAX was one of the first airports

454
00:26:21,861 --> 00:26:24,340
to have the fueling hydrants built into the aprons

455
00:26:24,341 --> 00:26:28,060
of the tarmac to facilitate underwing fueling,

456
00:26:28,061 --> 00:26:31,160
thus avoiding expensive collisions between jet wings

457
00:26:31,161 --> 00:26:31,960
and fueling carts.

458
00:26:31,961 --> 00:26:38,460
LAX incorporated a cutting edge baggage claim system built

459
00:26:38,461 --> 00:26:40,960
around the new carousel design.

460
00:26:40,961 --> 00:26:44,300
Luggage could now be loaded onto a conveyor belt that whisked

461
00:26:44,301 --> 00:26:47,460
it to a retrieval area, often arriving there before its owner

462
00:26:47,461 --> 00:26:49,460
did.

463
00:26:49,461 --> 00:26:53,960
A state of the art control tower 13 stories high set on top of

464
00:26:53,961 --> 00:26:57,960
the airport administration building, handling nearly 1,500

465
00:26:57,961 --> 00:27:02,760
flights a day and carrying roughly 33,000 people on three

466
00:27:02,860 --> 00:27:06,600
runways, each roughly two miles long.

467
00:27:06,700 --> 00:27:09,700
The sheer size of the equipment was daunting.

468
00:27:09,800 --> 00:27:12,800
Originally, they had three levels of nothing but

469
00:27:12,900 --> 00:27:14,200
electronics.

470
00:27:14,300 --> 00:27:16,720
And through the years, when the electronics improvements came

471
00:27:16,820 --> 00:27:20,320
along, they just reduced the size of the equipment down to

472
00:27:20,420 --> 00:27:23,620
where they didn't need all that space.

473
00:27:23,720 --> 00:27:27,160
After constructing a clean, efficient, subtle airport,

474
00:27:27,161 --> 00:27:30,960
the architects at LAX then planted an outlandish landmark

475
00:27:31,060 --> 00:27:33,800
smack in its center, whose main purpose

476
00:27:33,900 --> 00:27:39,700
was to symbolize the futuristic jet age theme of the airport.

477
00:27:39,800 --> 00:27:42,500
The overtly architectural restaurant and observation

478
00:27:42,600 --> 00:27:45,620
deck, with its spindly legs reminiscent of an alien

479
00:27:45,720 --> 00:27:50,420
spacecraft, was named the theme building.

480
00:27:50,520 --> 00:27:52,920
When LAX opened, projections indicated

481
00:27:53,020 --> 00:27:56,820
that it would never need more than 10,000 parking spaces.

482
00:27:56,821 --> 00:28:00,820
They were off by at least a multiple of three.

483
00:28:00,920 --> 00:28:03,760
Anticipating future needs is one of the most important and

484
00:28:03,860 --> 00:28:06,860
most difficult tasks for airport officials.

485
00:28:06,960 --> 00:28:10,260
Looking at how the market may respond not just five years from

486
00:28:10,360 --> 00:28:14,280
now but 20 years from now takes a tremendous amount of money and

487
00:28:14,380 --> 00:28:16,780
a lot of planning and environmental work to build a

488
00:28:16,880 --> 00:28:18,280
new hangar.

489
00:28:18,380 --> 00:28:21,620
In fact, these days just to add capacity to an airport, it takes

490
00:28:21,720 --> 00:28:25,620
almost 10 years just to build a new runway.

491
00:28:25,621 --> 00:28:29,340
The volume of air traffic is increasing at such a rate

492
00:28:29,341 --> 00:28:31,260
that planners can barely keep up.

493
00:28:31,960 --> 00:28:36,100
Over 635 million people traveled by plane in 1999,

494
00:28:36,800 --> 00:28:39,400
up 22 million over the previous year.

495
00:28:40,200 --> 00:28:42,440
The importance of airport support facilities

496
00:28:42,441 --> 00:28:43,600
is greater than ever.

497
00:28:44,680 --> 00:28:47,860
Older airports must adapt or fail.

498
00:28:49,440 --> 00:28:51,220
London's Heathrow International Airport

499
00:28:51,221 --> 00:28:52,340
is the world's busiest,

500
00:28:52,341 --> 00:28:54,440
with over 60 million passengers

501
00:28:54,441 --> 00:28:57,540
flying on over 90 airlines each year.

502
00:28:57,541 --> 00:29:00,240
International airports will return on Modern Marvels.

503
00:29:05,780 --> 00:29:09,180
We now return to International airports on Modern Marvels.

504
00:29:11,760 --> 00:29:15,560
At 53 square miles, Denver International Airport,

505
00:29:15,561 --> 00:29:20,100
or DIA, is one of the largest airports in the world.

506
00:29:20,101 --> 00:29:23,800
Completed in 1995, DIA is six times

507
00:29:23,801 --> 00:29:28,400
size of LaGuardia and JFK airports combined.

508
00:29:28,500 --> 00:29:32,140
It is more than twice the size of Manhattan Island.

509
00:29:32,240 --> 00:29:33,940
In fact, the entire city of Boston

510
00:29:34,040 --> 00:29:39,040
could fit within the confines of DIA.

511
00:29:39,140 --> 00:29:42,060
Approximately 23 miles from downtown Denver,

512
00:29:42,160 --> 00:29:46,320
it handles over 104,000 passengers each day,

513
00:29:46,420 --> 00:29:49,960
with over 1,371 flights taking off and landing

514
00:29:49,961 --> 00:29:52,960
on its five nearly 2 1⁄2-mile-long runways.

515
00:29:55,860 --> 00:29:59,200
One of the lines I heard that really defines that well

516
00:29:59,300 --> 00:30:02,900
is, how much metal can you pack in the sky?

517
00:30:03,000 --> 00:30:04,500
Constructing the massive airport

518
00:30:04,600 --> 00:30:09,220
took a roughly 10,000-person crew over five years.

519
00:30:09,320 --> 00:30:12,720
Situated on the plains of Colorado's eastern prairie,

520
00:30:12,820 --> 00:30:15,820
the task of leveling the land was an enormous job

521
00:30:15,920 --> 00:30:19,260
that encompassed shifting over 110 million cubic yards

522
00:30:19,261 --> 00:30:21,560
of Earth.

523
00:30:21,660 --> 00:30:25,660
Runways were built in layers, and over 2.5 million cubic

524
00:30:25,760 --> 00:30:29,400
yards of concrete were used to complete runways, taxiways,

525
00:30:29,500 --> 00:30:30,300
and aprons.

526
00:30:33,600 --> 00:30:37,180
Denver's flight control tower at 327 feet,

527
00:30:37,280 --> 00:30:40,420
the tallest in North America, was designed with the latest

528
00:30:40,520 --> 00:30:44,020
Doppler radar innovations to evaluate weather conditions

529
00:30:44,120 --> 00:30:46,760
in an environment that sees violent tornado spawning

530
00:30:46,761 --> 00:30:49,760
thunderstorms and icy, wind-shearing blizzards.

531
00:30:53,660 --> 00:30:56,600
DIA, like LaGuardia, divides loading and unloading

532
00:30:56,700 --> 00:31:01,400
passengers onto different floors to reduce congestion.

533
00:31:01,500 --> 00:31:05,120
Evoking the traveling lounges at Dulles, a light rail system

534
00:31:05,220 --> 00:31:07,820
at DIA takes passengers from their gates

535
00:31:07,920 --> 00:31:10,220
to the central terminal for picking up luggage

536
00:31:10,320 --> 00:31:14,860
and accessing ground transportation or parking lots.

537
00:31:14,861 --> 00:31:17,960
The central terminal is packed with so many retail outlets

538
00:31:18,060 --> 00:31:21,860
that DIA resembles a giant shopping mall.

539
00:31:21,960 --> 00:31:24,500
It's sort of a mini city in itself.

540
00:31:24,600 --> 00:31:27,840
There are 25,000 plus employees at the Denver airport,

541
00:31:27,940 --> 00:31:28,940
for instance.

542
00:31:29,040 --> 00:31:31,140
And so there is a need for everything

543
00:31:31,240 --> 00:31:34,880
you can think of from nurse stations, a doctor,

544
00:31:34,980 --> 00:31:39,220
a chapel, meeting spaces.

545
00:31:39,320 --> 00:31:42,960
DIA's architects not only made the airport gigantic,

546
00:31:42,961 --> 00:31:47,360
They went one step further and made it an icon, one of the most

547
00:31:47,460 --> 00:31:51,260
instantly recognizable airports ever built.

548
00:31:51,360 --> 00:31:54,700
We were very careful to try to incorporate something of this

549
00:31:54,800 --> 00:31:55,800
region.

550
00:31:55,900 --> 00:32:00,520
And so I searched long and hard for inspiration for something

551
00:32:00,620 --> 00:32:03,620
that said Colorado said the West.

552
00:32:03,720 --> 00:32:07,360
And the thing that, for me, that kept being there was the

553
00:32:07,460 --> 00:32:08,560
mountains.

554
00:32:08,660 --> 00:32:11,320
The mountains are very unique in this area of the country.

555
00:32:11,321 --> 00:32:15,120
And so the sort of ridge shapes or peaks and valleys that we

556
00:32:15,220 --> 00:32:18,920
created with the roof there was inspired by the mountains.

557
00:32:19,020 --> 00:32:23,360
And I think it kind of folds into the aspect of making

558
00:32:23,460 --> 00:32:27,460
buildings unique and different for the place.

559
00:32:27,560 --> 00:32:29,380
With an outer shell made of waterproof,

560
00:32:29,480 --> 00:32:33,180
Teflon-coated woven fiberglass, the roof membranes

561
00:32:33,280 --> 00:32:38,120
comprise over 15 acres of white peaks, which reflect 90% of the

562
00:32:38,220 --> 00:32:39,620
daylight.

563
00:32:39,621 --> 00:32:42,120
The remaining 10% of daylight sufficiently lights

564
00:32:42,121 --> 00:32:45,620
the interior of the terminal during the day.

565
00:32:45,621 --> 00:32:48,620
Amazingly, the roof membranes weigh less than two pounds

566
00:32:48,621 --> 00:32:52,960
per square foot and neither conduct nor store heat.

567
00:32:52,961 --> 00:32:58,180
The airport cuts quite a profile alone out on the planes.

568
00:32:58,181 --> 00:33:01,480
Incredibly, despite its size and newness,

569
00:33:01,481 --> 00:33:04,820
DIA is already becoming inadequate.

570
00:33:04,821 --> 00:33:07,380
Some people complain that there isn't enough parking

571
00:33:07,381 --> 00:33:10,280
and efforts have been made to increase the facilities.

572
00:33:10,380 --> 00:33:12,920
Others complain that it's too far from the city,

573
00:33:13,020 --> 00:33:16,120
and efforts are being made to design better access.

574
00:33:16,220 --> 00:33:18,720
Still others say it won't be able to handle increased

575
00:33:18,820 --> 00:33:24,040
traffic, up more than 20% since it opened its doors in 1995.

576
00:33:24,140 --> 00:33:26,740
Finally, some complain that there is always construction

577
00:33:26,840 --> 00:33:30,040
taking place, and not just at Denver,

578
00:33:30,140 --> 00:33:32,440
but practically everywhere.

579
00:33:32,540 --> 00:33:35,240
Airports never seem quite finished.

580
00:33:35,241 --> 00:33:37,740
One of the things that we have to keep in mind when we're

581
00:33:37,741 --> 00:33:41,040
designing an airport is that the life of the building is going

582
00:33:41,041 --> 00:33:44,760
to be over a long period of time, maybe 50 years, maybe

583
00:33:44,761 --> 00:33:45,461
longer.

584
00:33:45,860 --> 00:33:48,780
But over that 50-year period of time, we're going to see a lot

585
00:33:48,781 --> 00:33:49,481
of changes.

586
00:33:49,680 --> 00:33:52,940
And so to be able to create a building that has lots of

587
00:33:52,941 --> 00:33:56,960
flexibility in the future is paramount in the design of an

588
00:33:56,961 --> 00:33:57,661
airport.

589
00:33:59,680 --> 00:34:02,660
In Europe, expansion at 50-year-old Orly

590
00:34:02,661 --> 00:34:05,800
The International Airport in Paris did not prevent the need

591
00:34:05,801 --> 00:34:10,400
for a brand new airport, which opened in 1974 as Charles de

592
00:34:10,401 --> 00:34:13,020
Gaulle International Airport.

593
00:34:13,021 --> 00:34:16,860
Anticipating jumbo jets in its design, de Gaulle nevertheless

594
00:34:16,861 --> 00:34:23,360
expanded in 1983, 1989, and again in 1994.

595
00:34:23,361 --> 00:34:26,120
Closed to commercial flights during World War II,

596
00:34:26,121 --> 00:34:30,060
London's Gatwick reopened in the 1950s to steady growth and

597
00:34:30,061 --> 00:34:34,660
expansion despite continuing as a single runway airport.

598
00:34:34,661 --> 00:34:37,800
Today, Gatwick sees roughly 30 million passengers a year

599
00:34:37,801 --> 00:34:40,420
through its terminals.

600
00:34:40,421 --> 00:34:44,540
Amsterdam's Schiphol, which saw renovations in the 1960s, 1980s,

601
00:34:44,541 --> 00:34:48,420
and 1990s, also averages 30 million passengers a year.

602
00:34:52,140 --> 00:34:56,100
In America, JFK's airport redevelopment, the largest ever,

603
00:34:56,101 --> 00:35:00,420
will cost over $9 billion and includes a light rail system

604
00:35:00,421 --> 00:35:03,240
linked to the city's subway and railroad.

605
00:35:03,241 --> 00:35:08,320
New and renovated terminals will spruce up the aging facility.

606
00:35:08,321 --> 00:35:10,560
Dulles enlarged its main terminal,

607
00:35:10,561 --> 00:35:14,280
opened new concourses, and added gates to handle traffic, which

608
00:35:14,281 --> 00:35:19,200
rose 26% between 1998 and 1999 alone.

609
00:35:19,201 --> 00:35:22,860
Dulles is the fastest growing of the world's 50 largest airports.

610
00:35:26,100 --> 00:35:28,940
LAX plans to improve with freeway and railway

611
00:35:29,040 --> 00:35:33,140
extensions, a people mover, a new terminal, and more parking.

612
00:35:36,340 --> 00:35:38,740
LAX, like all airports, is imperfect.

613
00:35:42,440 --> 00:35:44,960
If you hang the name of the city on the airport,

614
00:35:45,060 --> 00:35:47,960
and the airport's a stinker, the city will take the rap.

615
00:35:48,060 --> 00:35:49,760
It can be out in the country.

616
00:35:49,860 --> 00:35:52,600
Sooner or later, the city will show up around the airport,

617
00:35:52,700 --> 00:35:54,100
and you'll have to deal with it.

618
00:35:54,101 --> 00:35:57,540
But from the get-go, the airport has to work.

619
00:35:57,640 --> 00:36:01,840
It has to be up and ready to roll.

620
00:36:01,940 --> 00:36:04,740
With all this activity going on, coordinating flights,

621
00:36:04,840 --> 00:36:07,640
travelers, and cargo, managing noise, expansion,

622
00:36:07,740 --> 00:36:10,620
and adaptability, you'd be surprised just how much

623
00:36:10,720 --> 00:36:12,620
your international airport is doing

624
00:36:12,720 --> 00:36:15,260
that you don't even know about.

625
00:36:15,360 --> 00:36:17,600
Charles de Gaulle International Airport in Paris

626
00:36:17,700 --> 00:36:21,520
has approximately 7,000 acres of land, nearly twice as much

627
00:36:21,521 --> 00:36:24,720
nearby Orly at 3,700 acres.

628
00:36:24,820 --> 00:36:26,120
Modern Marvels will return.

629
00:36:32,060 --> 00:36:35,360
We now return to international airports on Modern Marvels.

630
00:36:37,880 --> 00:36:39,980
Welcome to McCarran International Airport

631
00:36:40,080 --> 00:36:42,380
in Las Vegas, Nevada.

632
00:36:42,480 --> 00:36:44,280
Don't forget to drop a quarter on the slot machine

633
00:36:44,380 --> 00:36:46,780
on your way to your luggage.

634
00:36:46,880 --> 00:36:49,980
When visiting London's Gatwick, be sure to grab some English tea

635
00:36:49,981 --> 00:36:53,980
tea biscuits as you leave the gate.

636
00:36:54,080 --> 00:36:57,080
Every airport reflects the character of its place.

637
00:36:57,180 --> 00:37:00,020
But beneath the surface, international airports function

638
00:37:00,120 --> 00:37:04,120
in roughly the same way, using roughly the same language.

639
00:37:04,220 --> 00:37:07,840
They have to. There's a kind of choreography,

640
00:37:07,940 --> 00:37:10,840
a ballet, that has to take place.

641
00:37:10,940 --> 00:37:14,080
And the speed at which that takes place determines the

642
00:37:14,180 --> 00:37:17,640
number of passengers that you're able to service.

643
00:37:17,641 --> 00:37:25,160
It's such an immensely complicated task to make this system work, but it's not simply making one system work.

644
00:37:25,260 --> 00:37:28,360
It's making hundreds of systems work all over the country.

645
00:37:28,740 --> 00:37:36,800
The system at LAX has to work in similar ways to the system at LaGuardia or National or Dulles.

646
00:37:37,560 --> 00:37:42,080
Those interconnected systems help guarantee that your travel will be safe and pleasant.

647
00:37:42,780 --> 00:37:44,240
It begins with security.

648
00:37:45,000 --> 00:37:46,020
Lots of security.

649
00:37:46,021 --> 00:37:49,020
Control rooms full of agents monitor television feeds

650
00:37:49,021 --> 00:37:52,020
from cameras all over the airport,

651
00:37:52,021 --> 00:37:55,020
from curbside check-in to terminal gate.

652
00:37:55,021 --> 00:38:00,020
At entry to gate area security, multiple cameras capture

653
00:38:00,021 --> 00:38:03,020
different angles as passengers unload their belongings

654
00:38:03,021 --> 00:38:05,020
for the search and scan.

655
00:38:05,021 --> 00:38:09,020
This security gauntlet began in the early 70s,

656
00:38:09,021 --> 00:38:12,020
after a series of hijacking incidents prompted the Federal

657
00:38:12,021 --> 00:38:15,020
Aviation Administration to require that all airports

658
00:38:15,021 --> 00:38:17,260
All airports search travelers and their bags.

659
00:38:20,620 --> 00:38:24,660
Here at LAX, after a woman places her bags on the belt,

660
00:38:24,661 --> 00:38:28,640
a thief delays her from walking through the metal detector.

661
00:38:28,641 --> 00:38:30,660
On the other side, a different angle

662
00:38:30,661 --> 00:38:34,000
reveals his partner taking the woman's purse.

663
00:38:34,001 --> 00:38:37,980
This maneuver is known as a diversionary theft.

664
00:38:37,981 --> 00:38:40,280
Police were later able to retrieve the woman's purse

665
00:38:40,281 --> 00:38:43,980
by examining the security tape and identifying the suspects.

666
00:38:45,020 --> 00:38:49,020
High above the shops and restaurants, traffic control

667
00:38:49,120 --> 00:38:52,260
is managed within view of the runways.

668
00:38:52,360 --> 00:38:54,260
Controllers handle each of the planes

669
00:38:54,360 --> 00:38:57,760
that land every few seconds, every hour of every day

670
00:38:57,860 --> 00:38:59,760
of every year.

671
00:38:59,860 --> 00:39:03,280
Slips of paper represent every flight.

672
00:39:03,380 --> 00:39:06,180
Computers facilitate the communication between plane

673
00:39:06,280 --> 00:39:09,120
and tower, where the central controller gives the pilot

674
00:39:09,220 --> 00:39:10,680
his flight pattern.

675
00:39:10,780 --> 00:39:13,180
Another controller leads the plane out of the gate

676
00:39:13,181 --> 00:39:16,980
the gate and onto the taxiway, and a third guides the plane

677
00:39:17,080 --> 00:39:19,080
into the air.

678
00:39:19,180 --> 00:39:21,420
They are absolutely the most valuable element

679
00:39:21,520 --> 00:39:22,820
at the airport.

680
00:39:22,920 --> 00:39:25,020
We operate 2,000 operations here.

681
00:39:25,120 --> 00:39:28,720
That's 1,000 airplanes landing, 1,000 airplanes taking off

682
00:39:28,820 --> 00:39:30,040
on a daily basis.

683
00:39:30,140 --> 00:39:32,740
The timing, the preciseness, the scheduling of all that,

684
00:39:32,840 --> 00:39:37,340
and their ability to manage that is really quite amazing.

685
00:39:37,440 --> 00:39:39,540
Down on the tarmac, rescue operators

686
00:39:39,640 --> 00:39:42,240
rehearse for disaster.

687
00:39:42,241 --> 00:39:45,200
Airports have to expect the unexpected.

688
00:39:45,300 --> 00:39:47,680
Their efforts make airline travel one of the safest

689
00:39:47,780 --> 00:39:48,780
modes of transportation.

690
00:39:52,080 --> 00:39:54,980
Millions of people travel through customs every year

691
00:39:55,080 --> 00:39:57,880
at any given international airport.

692
00:39:57,980 --> 00:40:00,700
While customs officials use x-ray equipment to look for

693
00:40:00,800 --> 00:40:03,200
illegal imports, such as plants and animals,

694
00:40:03,300 --> 00:40:05,500
they also use dogs, like this beagle,

695
00:40:05,600 --> 00:40:06,600
to snip out contraband.

696
00:40:06,700 --> 00:40:07,600
Find it, girl.

697
00:40:07,700 --> 00:40:08,600
Find it.

698
00:40:08,700 --> 00:40:09,600
Where is it?

699
00:40:09,700 --> 00:40:11,600
The dog taps suspect baggage with its paws to signal

700
00:40:11,601 --> 00:40:16,900
suspicious, in this case, an orange from Turkey.

701
00:40:17,000 --> 00:40:19,140
This checkpoint makes agricultural inspectors

702
00:40:19,240 --> 00:40:22,240
happy, knowing that fruit or meat-borne toxins will find it

703
00:40:22,340 --> 00:40:24,940
difficult to get through the gauntlet.

704
00:40:25,040 --> 00:40:27,040
It makes the beagle happy too.

705
00:40:27,140 --> 00:40:29,160
He gets a treat every time he finds something.

706
00:40:32,460 --> 00:40:35,260
Dozens of airlines plan and adjust their schedules,

707
00:40:35,360 --> 00:40:37,560
while an army of mechanics check planes

708
00:40:37,660 --> 00:40:39,260
and conduct periodic tune-ups.

709
00:40:41,600 --> 00:40:46,000
Retail outlets bring in as much as $400 million in combined

710
00:40:46,001 --> 00:40:47,840
revenue at some airports.

711
00:40:47,841 --> 00:40:51,240
So much revenue that new airports today, like movie

712
00:40:51,241 --> 00:40:55,240
theaters, reap much of their profits from concessions.

713
00:40:55,241 --> 00:40:59,160
Some airports are focusing more on becoming a shopping mall

714
00:40:59,161 --> 00:41:02,560
than they are moving passengers in and out.

715
00:41:02,561 --> 00:41:05,160
And that's become a very important revenue source

716
00:41:05,161 --> 00:41:07,160
also for airports.

717
00:41:07,161 --> 00:41:10,560
Increasingly, airlines and airports are catering to

718
00:41:10,561 --> 00:41:14,160
are catering to non-human travelers.

719
00:41:14,260 --> 00:41:16,200
The biggest development at the airport

720
00:41:16,300 --> 00:41:17,560
is the air cargo business.

721
00:41:17,660 --> 00:41:19,600
We don't hear a lot about air cargo,

722
00:41:19,700 --> 00:41:24,200
but air cargo is the growing star in the transportation

723
00:41:24,300 --> 00:41:26,400
firmament.

724
00:41:26,500 --> 00:41:29,220
Perfectly successful airlines survive without ever

725
00:41:29,320 --> 00:41:31,420
boarding a paying customer.

726
00:41:31,520 --> 00:41:34,320
Instead, their planes are loaded with high priority letters

727
00:41:34,420 --> 00:41:39,260
and packages, or maybe Blue Crab from Chesapeake Bay.

728
00:41:39,261 --> 00:41:42,160
or the latest passions from the runways of Paris and Milan.

729
00:41:45,060 --> 00:41:47,160
Even the bodies of the recently deceased

730
00:41:47,260 --> 00:41:51,000
travel in the bellies of airplanes.

731
00:41:51,100 --> 00:41:52,800
Computerized tracking allows cargo

732
00:41:52,900 --> 00:41:55,500
to clear through customs, often before the plane touches

733
00:41:55,600 --> 00:41:58,620
down in a new country.

734
00:41:58,720 --> 00:42:02,720
The newest airports have to work within this overarching system,

735
00:42:02,820 --> 00:42:06,060
even as they look for ways to improve on the past.

736
00:42:06,160 --> 00:42:08,120
This puts quite a burden on the architects,

737
00:42:08,121 --> 00:42:11,420
who design airports such as the new Incheon Airport in Seoul,

738
00:42:11,520 --> 00:42:14,220
South Korea.

739
00:42:14,320 --> 00:42:16,920
Right now, Korean Airlines is the second largest

740
00:42:17,020 --> 00:42:19,460
air cargo carrier in the world.

741
00:42:19,560 --> 00:42:24,360
And the development of Incheon will further move that number

742
00:42:24,460 --> 00:42:28,680
along, because the China market is everybody's goal.

743
00:42:28,780 --> 00:42:31,620
And Incheon will become an important trans gateway

744
00:42:31,720 --> 00:42:37,320
to the entire Asia-Pacific market.

745
00:42:37,321 --> 00:42:40,720
The airport is built on a man-made land bridge between two

746
00:42:40,721 --> 00:42:46,320
islands in the Yellow Sea, about 30 miles from downtown Seoul.

747
00:42:46,321 --> 00:42:49,560
The islands create a natural barrier to rough seas, which

748
00:42:49,561 --> 00:42:53,960
might otherwise make the location a forbidding site.

749
00:42:53,961 --> 00:42:56,680
The remote location also mitigates the effects of noise

750
00:42:56,681 --> 00:42:59,280
pollution.

751
00:42:59,281 --> 00:43:03,080
Fentra's Bradburn architects, who built Denver's airport, won a

752
00:43:03,081 --> 00:43:06,920
design competition to build the 5.6 million square foot central

753
00:43:06,921 --> 00:43:10,920
passenger terminal. They began with a model.

754
00:43:10,921 --> 00:43:13,920
In the design of buildings, we use models extensively for

755
00:43:13,921 --> 00:43:19,920
creation of the vision to help us illustrate and communicate to

756
00:43:19,921 --> 00:43:23,920
our clients and to the public at large what it is we're trying

757
00:43:23,921 --> 00:43:25,920
to do. And they're particularly

758
00:43:25,921 --> 00:43:29,920
interesting and helpful whenever we have something that is unique

759
00:43:29,921 --> 00:43:35,920
in terms of its spatial quality or the form of the building.

760
00:43:35,921 --> 00:43:40,720
The $6.9 billion airport includes 2.3-mile-long

761
00:43:40,820 --> 00:43:45,060
runways, two passenger terminals, and four remote

762
00:43:45,160 --> 00:43:51,260
concourses that will handle 27 million passengers a year.

763
00:43:51,360 --> 00:43:56,500
Inchon's control tower is 329 and 1 3rd feet high, 3 and 1

764
00:43:56,600 --> 00:43:59,340
half feet taller than Denver's.

765
00:43:59,440 --> 00:44:03,040
The new airport is within 3 and 1 half hours flying time of 40

766
00:44:03,041 --> 00:44:08,340
major metropolitan areas in the dynamic Pacific Rim.

767
00:44:08,440 --> 00:44:10,740
Undoubtedly, the airport's designers have left room for

768
00:44:10,840 --> 00:44:13,980
changes and adjustments, for nobody really knows exactly

769
00:44:14,080 --> 00:44:16,280
where advancements in technology and engineering

770
00:44:16,380 --> 00:44:18,780
are going to lead.

771
00:44:18,880 --> 00:44:22,500
Amazingly, the next leap may be straight out of the atmosphere

772
00:44:22,600 --> 00:44:24,400
and into space.

773
00:44:24,500 --> 00:44:27,040
The Federal Aviation Administration already has as

774
00:44:27,140 --> 00:44:31,440
part of its mission the space program on the commercial side.

775
00:44:31,441 --> 00:44:34,640
And so, you know, we're looking at spaceports,

776
00:44:34,740 --> 00:44:37,320
incorporating that with runways and launch facilities.

777
00:44:37,620 --> 00:44:39,760
Someday we might be traveling to Mars,

778
00:44:39,761 --> 00:44:41,920
and the airports will then become spaceports.

779
00:44:43,480 --> 00:44:47,560
International airports may someday become interplanetary ones.

780
00:44:48,720 --> 00:44:51,840
It's hard to imagine, but who could have predicted an airport

